[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I think I just found the best PowerToys alternative for Mac. If you don\'t know what Microsoft PowerToys is, it\'s basically a bunch of useful utilities that fix annoying problems all in one app for Windows. The Mac equivalent of this is called Versant, not Crossant, Versant. The nice thing about it is that it\'s modular, so you'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can just install the tools you actually need and uninstall the ones you don\'t, and it\'s free. So, about a month ago, I decided to give it a try, and after using it for quite a while, I think I have some thoughts. Let\'s start with the tool that I actually use the most. If'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve seen my MacBook review, you\'ll know exactly what I\'m talking about. If you haven\'t, here\'s the rundown. If you want to use your Mac with an external monitor in clamshell mode, so with the lid closed, you have to have it connected to the charger. And as soon as'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you unplug it, the Mac goes to sleep. Keep awake fixes that. You have two options. One to turn keep awake on, and another to keep it going with the lid closed. That\'s the one I always have enabled. Though, there is a built-in terminal command, caffeinate, that can keep your Mac awake, but then when I'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'close the lid, the external monitor also turns off. So, keep awake it is. It also lets you customize the icon and lets you change how it behaves when you have it on. Then, there\'s the volume mixer. For context, on Windows, you can go to the action center and change the volume for'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'each app that you have open. On Mac, there is no such option until now. I\'ve been using it quite a lot while editing. I have a Spotify playing through the built-in speakers and the sound from the editor in my headphones. So, I can listen to music while also hearing the'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'voiceover instead of just sitting in complete silence while I\'m importing files or creating animations. Mouse fixes and controls. Oh, this is a big one. By default, it feels like macOS is in a toxic relationship with external'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mice. If you come from Windows and plug a mouse into the Mac, you will know that something is completely wrong straight away. The scrolling will feel very choppy and it will be in a completely opposite direction. Apple, hear me out. A mouse and a trackpad are two'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completely different input devices. They\'re not the same object. Why do two different settings do the same thing? Anyways, Versant fixes that. It can invert the scrolling just for the mouse, so scrolling down is down and scrolling'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up is up, like every other operating system. And it can even make it smooth. So, external mice can finally be usable with macOS. The screenshot tool and the copy text from screen are also so much better than the built-in ones. The'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'screenshot tool is great. It feels like something you would actually pay for as it lets you do so much more stuff than the default preview app. And the text extractor tool is great since I don\'t have to do a screenshot every time I can\'t copy a bunch of text from an'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image. Instead, I can just highlight what I want and boom, it\'s in my clipboard. Finally, the cut and paste is something I didn\'t think I would actually use. For context, on Mac, if you want to move a file from one folder to another, you have to first copy it and then in the'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'final folder, press command option V to move it there. What F key does is it brings back the normal Windows and Linux style file cut and paste shortcuts. So, they can be command X and command V. And honestly, I didn\'t think I would use it'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'much, but now that I have it, my brain just automatically presses it. Quit and close. This is another thing that I complained about in the previous video. Basically, some apps like Obsidian, Notes, and Chrome will stay open even if I close the only window.'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Quit and close fixes that. So, now instead of using command Q to quit apps, I can just click the red X on the last open window and the app will quit. This is one thing that Windows does better in my opinion. Stats can also be very useful. They can'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'either live in the menu bar like mine or you can have yours be in a separate menu. They\'re very interesting to look at when you\'re launching heavy apps or have a lot of stuff open. The network tab also shows you your bandwidth and you can do a speed test right from the'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'menu. There\'s also battery info with real-time power usage and battery health. There\'s also a separate menu that shows you the disk\'s health, stats, and usage right there. Plus, you have quick toggles for things like switching to light mode, turning off the keyboard'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'backlight, emptying the trash, ejecting all disks, etc. Okay, lightning round. Cleaning mode basically locks the keyboard and trackpad so you don\'t accidentally press anything while cleaning your Mac. It\'s great if you have a MacBook Pro with the'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'black keyboard. That thing shows dust very quickly. Command bar is a spotlight alternative with ability to use for some tools right from it. Though I prefer normal spotlight as I haven\'t found it to do anything extra for my use. Quick panel is a floating panel with your'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'favorite tools and shortcuts. Again, use it maybe once or twice. Uninstaller is literally what it sounds like. It uninstalls the app with its cache, logs, and whatever leftovers it might leave behind. Cleaner deletes app leftovers, cache, and logs if you uninstall the app'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'normally. Homebrew lets you install and update Homebrew packages without the terminal. Clean URL removes tracking from copied links. And there\'s so much more stuff that I haven\'t even mentioned just because I either haven\'t tried it or tried it but didn\'t find much use of'

[04:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it for myself. Though there is one thing that I miss from PowerToys and that\'s always on top windows. You don\'t understand how annoying importing and saving files can be until you start editing videos and coding. Resone fixes'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the file part of the equation. It has a floating shelf where I can drag files temporarily until I get to my desired app and then drag them in from the panel. Anyways, Resone has replaced like three separate apps for me. So if you haven\'t'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tried it and you have the same craps with macOS as me, give it a go. It\'s free. I\'ve had like three updates already that brought more features. But for now, like and subscribe. See you.'
